1
00:00:02,254 --> 00:00:10,628
Google just took the number one spot in AI back from Anthropic and OpenAI, and almost nobody on the planet is allowed to use the model that did it.

2
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:12,708
It's called Gemini 4 Carbon.

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:22,212
We've been following it since it first leaked, and on October 28th, Google finally made it official, about three hours before Alphabet's quarterly earnings call.

4
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:33,860
Within hours, it was sitting at the top of the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index, the closest thing this industry has to a neutral scoreboard, ahead of Claude Opus 5.5 and ahead of GPT-6 Astra.

5
00:00:34,286 --> 00:00:38,000
Influencers started calling it the greatest Jedi counterattack of the year.

6
00:00:38,286 --> 00:00:44,164
Then people went to try it and found out the only ones with access are a small group of cybersecurity defenders.

7
00:00:44,814 --> 00:00:52,196
So the smartest AI in the world right now, at least on paper, is sitting in a locked room and Google won't say when the door opens.

8
00:00:52,846 --> 00:00:57,124
The timing is kind of funny because the earnings were a lot less exciting than the model.

9
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:09,124
Alphabet's free cash flow for the quarter came in at a loss of just over $9 billion, the second negative quarter in a row, and the stock fell more than 10% in after hours trading at one point.

10
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:16,260
Carbon, by the way, is the element that turns into diamond under enough pressure, so apparently an earnings call counts.

11
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:18,628
The other awkward part is Argon.

12
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:26,052
Just two weeks earlier, Google opened Gemini 4 Argon to paying users, and we covered that launch as Google's big new flagship.

13
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:28,772
It got to be the flagship for about 15 days.

14
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:36,708
People online had a field day with the names, with one person joking that Google went from Argon to Carbon in two weeks, so iron must be next.

15
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:38,852
And after that, it's time for heat treatment.

16
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:47,588
Plenty of people said carbon is so much better that they wish Google had skipped Argon entirely, because the comeback would have been way more convincing.

17
00:01:48,046 --> 00:01:53,380
Peter Gostev, who heads capability evaluation at Arena, said pretty much that exact thing.

18
00:01:53,934 --> 00:01:55,268
So, the actual numbers.

19
00:01:55,534 --> 00:02:00,740
On version 5.0 of the Artificial Analysis Index, Carbon scored 59.

20
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:03,812
Claude Opus, 5.5, is at 58.

21
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,796
GPT 6, Astra at 57, and Argon, from two weeks ago, scored 50.

22
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:16,036
That's a nine point jump in two weeks, and the typical model on that chart sits around 42.

23
00:02:16,526 --> 00:02:24,836
The last time Google was on top was back in April, when Gemini 3.1 Pro tied with GPT 5.4 and Claude Opus 4.7.

24
00:02:25,326 --> 00:02:31,716
Then OpenAI and Anthropic kept shipping, and for about half a year, Google kind of got treated as the third-place lab.

25
00:02:32,174 --> 00:02:36,164
That index is built from nine different tests, and Carbon won four of them.

26
00:02:36,494 --> 00:02:41,508
The ones on tough reasoning, scientific research, knowledge questions, and automated tasks.

27
00:02:42,222 --> 00:02:44,000
Google's own charts look even better.

28
00:02:44,302 --> 00:02:52,996
Out of 14 core benchmark tables Google published, carbon took first in 12, and across a wider set, it won 15 out of 17.

29
00:02:53,454 --> 00:02:58,480
It also took first on another independent ranking, the Val's Index, with 70.4%.

30
00:02:59,246 --> 00:03:09,796
And on Arena, where regular people vote on answers blind without knowing which AI wrote them, Carbon hit 1,528 points on the main text leaderboard, number one in the world.

31
00:03:10,222 --> 00:03:14,276
A jump that big, that fast, brought the RSI question right back.

32
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:18,532
Recursive self-improvement, AI helping build the next version of AI.

33
00:03:19,022 --> 00:03:32,612
And Google kind of invited it because in the official announcement, Kore Kavukuoglu, an SVP at Google DeepMind and Google's chief AI architect, wrote that their researchers and models are working hand in hand to advance capabilities faster.

34
00:03:33,038 --> 00:03:36,480
That's the only line on it, but you can see why people ran with it.

35
00:03:36,654 --> 00:03:38,852
One of the biggest upgrades is long memory.

36
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:49,476
Carbon can take in about a million tokens at once, tokens being chunks of words, which works out to around 1,500 printed pages, and it reads text, images, speech, and video.

37
00:03:49,902 --> 00:03:52,292
The hard part is keeping track of all of it.

38
00:03:52,686 --> 00:04:00,800
On a test that hides connections across up to a million tokens and asks the model to follow them, Carbon scored 96.2%.

39
00:04:01,582 --> 00:04:10,916
Argon got 84.2%, GPT 6, Astra 71.8%, and Claude Opus 5.566.8%.

40
00:04:11,918 --> 00:04:15,844
So you can bury it in documents, and it pretty much never loses the thread.

41
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:18,212
It's also really strong on the brainy stuff.

42
00:04:18,574 --> 00:04:23,780
It scored 80% on a science and math test called Riemann Bench, way ahead of everyone.

43
00:04:24,366 --> 00:04:34,768
On HLE Diamond, which is basically a brutal final exam across tons of subjects, it's first with 69%, one point ahead of Opus, 5.5%.

44
00:04:35,342 --> 00:04:41,488
On a test where the AI does real scientific research work on a computer, it's first again at about 65%.

45
00:04:42,318 --> 00:04:44,448
Coding has always been Google's weak spot.

46
00:04:44,622 --> 00:05:00,836
On Terminal Bench 4.0, which tests how well an AI handles long programming jobs in that black text window programmers type into, Carbon jumped from Argon's 57.1% to 65.2%, which Google lists as first place.

47
00:05:01,454 --> 00:05:11,236
On Artificial Analysis's coding agent ranking, Carbon scored 59, second only to Opus 5.5 at 62, the closest Google has ever gotten there.

48
00:05:11,502 --> 00:05:21,124
Claude and GPT still win some of the hardcore software engineering tests though, like Frontier SWE2 and OS World, which is about actually operating a computer.

49
00:05:21,582 --> 00:05:23,236
Building websites got a big boost.

50
00:05:23,662 --> 00:05:30,692
On WebDev Arena, Carbon jumped 89 points over Argon to fourth place with 1,768.

51
00:05:31,214 --> 00:05:36,356
Google's own post says 1,769, so somebody's off by one.

52
00:05:36,814 --> 00:05:41,860
Where Carbon really pulls ahead is anything it has to run by itself for a long time.

53
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:51,332
On Vending Bench 2, where the AI runs a simulated business over a long stretch, Carbon scored 1.8 times what Claude Opus 5.5 got.

54
00:05:51,568 --> 00:06:02,692
On Arena's Agent Leaderboard, it jumps straight to second in the world, behind only Opus 5.5 Max and ahead of Sonnet 5.5 Max and GPT-6 AstraMax.

55
00:06:03,374 --> 00:06:12,768
That leaderboard even measures how well a model adjusts when you change your mind halfway through or push back on something, and carbon is first there at plus 18.6%.

56
00:06:13,614 --> 00:06:19,780
Each agent task cost about $1.80, 72% less than Opus 5.5 Max.

57
00:06:20,366 --> 00:06:33,476
Google says that internally carbon has worked for days at a time inside giant code bases and low-level systems, hitting dead ends, trying something else, and grinding until it finds the root cause of some subtle bug and patches it.

58
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:36,068
Testers noticed an attitude change too.

59
00:06:36,494 --> 00:06:43,684
An early tester who goes by lentils said it's way less lazy than before, more proactive, and less likely to just go through the motions.

60
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:50,788
Some people got their hands on it early through testing programs and blind tests on Arena, and the demos are honestly gorgeous.

61
00:06:51,342 --> 00:07:05,168
On October 21st, a reviewer named Lumina asked it to build a 3D Chinese pagoda out of Minecraft style blocks, with full light and shadow in one go, and ran the same prompt through four models side by side.

62
00:07:05,582 --> 00:07:08,288
Argon's pagoda came out thin and rough.

63
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:19,248
Carbon's had properly proportioned layers of overhanging roofs, water with real reflections, and a landscaped courtyard, basically on the level of Claude Opus 5.5.

64
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:23,328
Then there's the classic test: a pelican riding a bicycle, drawn in code.

65
00:07:23,662 --> 00:07:34,688
Carbon drew a really detailed pelican, added a slider to change the pedaling speed anywhere from 20 to 100 RPM, and put a button in the corner labeled CAD Engineering Blueprint Inspection.

66
00:07:34,848 --> 00:07:42,372
Click it, and the whole thing flips into a dark blue X ray blueprint, showing the joints in the pelican's legs and the gear ratio of the bike.

67
00:07:43,054 --> 00:07:46,916
So, if you ever needed the gear ratio of a pelican, Google has you covered.

68
00:07:47,374 --> 00:07:52,228
A developer called Rafi2code posted a peacock spreading its tail.

69
00:07:52,526 --> 00:08:01,028
Other people shared 3D cities, they say look more natural than anything else out there, and some reviewers said they were genuinely shocked by a black hole it made.

70
00:08:01,550 --> 00:08:05,412
Lentils did point out that regular website and app design still isn't great.

71
00:08:05,838 --> 00:08:07,300
The biggest complaint is speed.

72
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:16,132
Lumina said that depending on the task, it's two to four times slower than Argon, and testers keep saying it thinks two to four times longer before answering.

73
00:08:16,622 --> 00:08:22,756
Now, artificial analysis found it actually writes faster than average, around 100 tokens per second.

74
00:08:22,992 --> 00:08:24,388
It just has a lot to say.

75
00:08:24,878 --> 00:08:33,284
To get through their tests, it produced 200 million tokens, while the typical model used 64 million, so each task took it around 21 minutes.

76
00:08:33,712 --> 00:08:37,380
Carbon is that coworker who answers a yes or no question with a slide deck.

77
00:08:37,774 --> 00:08:40,260
The testing company Valsai saw the same thing.

78
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:44,612
On finance and tax tasks, it took more than twice as long as Argon.

79
00:08:44,912 --> 00:08:51,872
And on finance, it actually got slightly less accurate, 67.66% down to 67.16%.

80
00:08:52,558 --> 00:08:59,716
It won their legal research test, but it averaged almost 52 minutes per task, about 20 minutes longer than Argon.

81
00:09:00,206 --> 00:09:03,940
The other big fight is over hallucination, the AI making stuff up.

82
00:09:04,302 --> 00:09:09,572
One person on X said that until Google seriously cuts it, they don't care how many points carbon wins.

83
00:09:10,254 --> 00:09:21,072
On Artificial Analysis's Knowledge Test, Carbon's accuracy went up from 48% to 58%, but its hallucination rate went from 4% to 11%.

84
00:09:21,806 --> 00:09:28,580
That number means, out of the questions it got wrong, how often it made up an answer, instead of admitting it didn't know.

85
00:09:28,942 --> 00:09:31,300
So that's a real step back from Argon.

86
00:09:31,758 --> 00:09:36,676
At the same time, 11% is still the lowest of any model on that chart.

87
00:09:36,974 --> 00:09:42,512
And Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra are both around 50%.

88
00:09:43,246 --> 00:09:50,916
On the combined score that rewards right answers and punishes made up ones, carbon is first, 54 to Opus's 48.

89
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:59,716
Google's own testing says Carbon makes about half as many unsupported claims as Argon, which is a different kind of test, so both can be true.

90
00:10:00,174 --> 00:10:01,796
Okay, so why the locked room?

91
00:10:02,158 --> 00:10:02,916
Cybersecurity.

92
00:10:03,246 --> 00:10:05,476
Defending software comes down to three jobs.

93
00:10:05,870 --> 00:10:10,468
Finding the holes, figuring out which ones an attacker could actually use, and fixing them.

94
00:10:10,926 --> 00:10:12,912
Carbon set new records on all three.

95
00:10:13,134 --> 00:10:17,632
On finding real vulnerabilities in real software, it scored 88.2%.

96
00:10:18,318 --> 00:10:26,112
On proving whether a vulnerability can actually be exploited, which defenders use to decide what to fix first, it hit 54.8%.

97
00:10:26,830 --> 00:10:29,552
And on writing the actual fix, 71%.

98
00:10:30,254 --> 00:10:33,828
Google says those were measured without guardrails, so that's the raw model.

99
00:10:34,254 --> 00:10:42,308
Google's Chrome team says carbon was more than 10 times better at writing high-quality security patches than another lab's big frontier model.

100
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:49,508
Wiz, Google's cloud security company, says it leads their website hacking tests for a project called Scan for Good.

101
00:10:49,902 --> 00:10:54,564
And internally, its success rate at writing secure code is more than double argons.

102
00:10:54,912 --> 00:11:01,124
Obviously, a model that's this good at finding and exploiting holes is just as handy for the people attacking systems.

103
00:11:01,806 --> 00:11:15,620
So this week it goes to a small group of cyber defense organizations, then slowly to trusted partners through something called the Fairwind program, and after that to developers, businesses, and regular people as soon as possible, with no date.

104
00:11:15,950 --> 00:11:21,380
Google is also part of the US government's voluntary program for testing models before release.

105
00:11:21,998 --> 00:11:24,512
Google also put a lot of effort into behavior.

106
00:11:24,846 --> 00:11:30,436
Prompt injection is when hidden instructions on a webpage or in an email try to hijack the AI.

107
00:11:30,894 --> 00:11:37,220
On Grayswan's test, attackers get up to 15 tries, and their success rate against carbon was zero.

108
00:11:37,472 --> 00:11:38,660
Every attack failed.

109
00:11:39,182 --> 00:11:47,684
Argon was already leading with 0.7%, Opus 5.5 got 1%, and GPT-6 Astra 8.5%.

110
00:11:48,302 --> 00:11:51,044
Google is even calling Carbon an honest model.

111
00:11:51,392 --> 00:12:03,072
Some AIs exploit loopholes in tests to fake a good score, and on Terminal Bench 4.0, Carbon's successful cheating dropped to 0%, with attempts down to 0.4%.

112
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:08,672
Working with Google's own coding teams, serious misbehavior alerts dropped by almost 80%.

113
00:12:09,390 --> 00:12:14,032
Overstepping, doing things it wasn't asked to, fell 26%.

114
00:12:14,766 --> 00:12:16,912
And underdelivering fell 29%.

115
00:12:17,614 --> 00:12:34,820
Google trained it to handle complicated, high-pressure jobs properly, so when it's missing information, it writes the caveats straight into the work, or stops and lists exactly what's still needed, and when your request is vague, it pulls the relevant sources and flags conflicts to you before acting.

116
00:12:35,182 --> 00:12:40,484
These numbers come from specific test conditions, but Google clearly wants the AI to stop cutting corners.

117
00:12:41,070 --> 00:12:52,164
As for price, Carbon launches at Argonne's introductory price: $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, and anything it has already seen gets 95% off.

118
00:12:52,622 --> 00:13:01,156
After the promo ends, it goes to $4 and $20, right where Claude Opus $5.5 is priced, and Google hasn't said when that happens.

119
00:13:01,806 --> 00:13:13,348
At full price, artificial analysis says carbon costs about $7.76 per task, basically the same as Argon's $7.68 while scoring 9 points higher.

120
00:13:13,806 --> 00:13:18,200
That's about 23% cheaper than Opus 5.5 at $10.04.

121
00:13:18,926 --> 00:13:24,640
But GPT-6 Astra comes in about 20% cheaper than Carbon at $6.24.

122
00:13:25,422 --> 00:13:32,388
Because it's so wordy, artificial analysis still calls it somewhat expensive, 45th out of 51 on cost.

123
00:13:33,038 --> 00:13:44,196
On Hacker News, developers are annoyed that Google always rushes to announce models but never gives a public date, and by the time Carbon opens up, OpenAI and Anthropic might already have their next models out.

124
00:13:44,654 --> 00:13:52,164
My favorite take compared Google to a guy who keeps bragging about his girlfriend, except she goes to another school, so nobody's ever met her.

125
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:55,520
Let me know in the comments if you think Google is actually back.

126
00:13:55,860 --> 00:13:57,700
Thanks for watching, and I'll catch you in the next one.

